From d4f09ef0e414c64b18c7417f950e46381a7e5159 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Andrey Stoyan <andrey.stoyan.csam@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 15 Aug 2026 02:42:46 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 07/44] feat(chexc): capability list aliases (proposal 6.1)

A tuple of exceptions used in a clause is a capability list: `throws MyExceptions`
where `type MyExceptions = (A, B)` means `throws A, B`.

- One shared helper (TypeCheckUtil::ExpandCapabilityList) splices tuple entries
  into the list, recursively, so aliases of aliases work. It resolves an entry
  that is still the alias itself as well as one already resolved to its target:
  declaration clauses and functional types elaborate at different points and
  present the entry in different forms. A visited set guards cyclic aliases,
  which the type checker reports separately.
- Wired into all three elaboration paths: declaration `throws` clauses,
  `captures` clauses (6.1 applies there too, per 3.9), and functional types.
- Clause validation now applies the per-entry rules (subtype of Exception, no
  concrete unchecked type) to the EXPANDED elements, so a bad type inside an
  alias tuple is diagnosed. Directly written entries keep their source-text
  spelling in diagnostics; alias elements, which have no source of their own,
  are named by their type.

Gate: CheckedExceptions aliases 4/4, all other implemented groups unchanged
(parse 11, types 10, scopes 7, captures 6, unchecked 4, inference 6); ctest
49/49; Parser/Sema LLT 3934/3934; clang-format clean.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
